import{_ as a,d as t,e as o,x as n}from"./chunks/framework.BRr8VP3a.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","xkChars":4,"xkWords":755},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/index.md","filePath":"en/theory/index.md"}'),r={name:"en/theory/index.md"};function i(s,e,p,d,l,h){return t(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="book" tabindex="-1">Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation <a class="header-anchor" href="#book" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation {#book}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>&gt; Original text © 2026 Xikari · English translation of v2 (November 2026) · <a href="https://xi-kari.com/en/works/xikari-conditions/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">https://xi-kari.com/en/works/xikari-conditions/</a> &gt; &gt; This book is released under the MIT License. When copying or distributing all or a substantial portion of it, keep the copyright notice and the license text.</p><p>By Xikari</p><p>Objects, Conditions, Effects, and Bounded Action</p><p>v2 · November 2026</p><h2 id="reading-scope" tabindex="-1">Scope and Reading Approach <a class="header-anchor" href="#reading-scope" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Scope and Reading Approach {#reading-scope}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This book examines how empirical objects form structures, the relations through which they operate, and how conditions and events change their states. A structural judgment always concerns a specific object, question, time window, and set of available materials. Whether different judgments can be connected depends on conditions concerning representation, evidence, mechanisms, resources, and permissions. The book develops structural explanation, event projection, conditional foresight, and bounded choice in separate treatments. This allows bodies of domain knowledge to be checked against one another while retaining their own objects and methods. The common thread is to identify which conditions support objects and relations, which effects can be transmitted across periods, and which distinctions must be preserved in conversions of representation and action.</p><p>The universal layer addresses the empirical world in a broad sense. Human action, meaning, responsibility, and authorization enter their respective specialized layers. They must not be projected back onto natural processes as a shared purpose. Actors and multi-circle configurations are represented jointly only when the representation can preserve the distinctions needed for the task.<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn1" id="fnref1">[1]</a></sup> Understanding may be an end in itself. Well-grounded local answers need not wait for every question to be resolved, nor does complexity constitute explanatory or foresight capability.<sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn2" id="fnref2">[2]</a></sup></p><p>Judgments about the future require the target, horizon, baseline, and failure conditions to be stated. Recommendations require normative premises and the price to be paid to be disclosed. Actual adoption and execution also require the applicable protections, review, and authorization. Definitions, formal proofs, teaching scenarios, domain experience, and effects of actual use each bear their own evidential responsibilities; one must not stand in for evidence for another.</p><p>Parts 1 and 2 explain the reading approach and evidential responsibilities. Parts 3–6 develop the universal grammar, conditional propositions, empirical testing, scale transformations, and mechanisms of operation. Parts 7–11 address human experience, collective prototypes, actors, multi-circle configurations, and dynamic projection. Parts 12–16 discuss foresight, choice, tools, norms, intervention, and governance. Appendix A provides the full interfaces for human variables. Appendix B provides a terminology index. Appendix C presents the common method and self-contained formal arguments. Appendix D connects explanations and applications across 32 domains.</p><h2 id="contents" tabindex="-1">Contents by Part <a class="header-anchor" href="#contents" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Contents by Part {#contents}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-01.html#part_1">Part 1 Introduction</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-02.html#part_2">Part 2 Boundaries and Methods</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-03.html#part_3">Part 3 Universal Structural Grammar</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-04.html#part_4">Part 4 Empirical Questions, Outcome Contracts, and Conditional Formal Propositions</a><sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn3" id="fnref3">[3]</a></sup></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-05.html#part_5">Part 5 Cross-Scale and Cross-Circle Transformations</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-06.html#part_6">Part 6 Operation and Evolution</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-07.html#part_7">Part 7 The Human Structured World</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-08.html#part_8">Part 8 Human State Prototypes</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-09.html#part_9">Part 9 Actor States and Personality Hypotheses</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-10.html#part_10">Part 10 Multi-Circle Objects and Joint States</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-11.html#part_11">Part 11 Event-Driven Dynamic Projection</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-12.html#part_12">Part 12 Conditional Foresight and Bounded Choice</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-13.html#part_13">Part 13 Interfaces and Tools</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-14.html#part_14">Part 14 Normative Choice</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-15.html#part_15">Part 15 Intervention and Applications</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/part-16.html#part_16">Part 16 Governance</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/appendix-a/#part_17">Appendix A Human Variable Interface Cards</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/appendix-b.html#part_18">Appendix B Numbering Scheme and Glossary</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/appendix-c.html#part_19">Appendix C Common Method Kernel and Cross-Disciplinary Bridging</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/appendix-d/#part_20">Appendix D A Map of Domain Explanations and Applications</a></p><p><a href="/xi-kari-skill/handbook/en/theory/references.html#part_21">References</a></p><p>The book&#39;s basic commitment is to provide an analytical language that can be checked and arguments that hold under stated conditions. It does not presuppose that the four families of empirical questions suffice to deduce all domain knowledge. Proofs of formal propositions, support from instance evidence, and the practical benefits of the method as a whole each have their own standards of judgment. As the range of application expands, domain mechanisms may be added, or a simpler and sufficient method may take over.</p></div><hr class="footnotes-sep"><section class="footnotes"><ol class="footnotes-list"><li id="fn1" class="footnote-item"><p>Translator&#39;s note: Circles (圈层) may be parallel, nested, overlapping, or bridged. <a href="#fnref1" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li><li id="fn2" class="footnote-item"><p>Translator&#39;s note: Here, foresight requires registration of a target, horizon, and baseline, as well as calibration; it is more restrictive than scenario planning. <a href="#fnref2" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li><li id="fn3" class="footnote-item"><p>Translator&#39;s note: A contract (合同) is an agreement registered and frozen before results are known, closer to pre-registration than to a legal document. <a href="#fnref3" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li></ol></section>',3)])])}const m=a(r,[["render",i]]);export{f as __pageData,m as default};
